### Description

When Plotly.react is called to update a plot that has a contour trace with autocontour true, the contour size/min/max are all calculated based on the previous contour trace z values.  This can result in no contours being drawn or incorrect ones.  This happens even though the data arrays are different instances.

How it happens:
in plots/plots.js method supplyDefaults:
```js
if(oldFullData.length === newFullData.length) {
        for(i = 0; i < newFullData.length; i++) {
            relinkPrivateKeys(newFullData[i], oldFullData[i]);
        }
    }
```
copies the _zmin/_zmax from the previous trace to the new trace data

in traces/contour/set_contours.js
the trace zmin/zmax are used to calculate the contour size/min/max

It is not until after setContours is called in 
traces/contour/calc.js
that a call is made to 
Colorscale.calc(gd, trace, {vals: cVals, cLetter: 'z'});
that the correct trace.zmin/zmax are filled in.  

Setting the trace.zmin/zmax to the correct values before the call to Plotly.react does not solve the problem.  Right now the workaround is to set _zmin/_zmax to undefined before the call to react:
```ts
const fullData = (this.plotDiv() as any)._fullData;
		if (fullData && fullData.length > 0) {
			for (const trace of fullData) {
				if (trace.type === 'contour') {
					trace.zmin = undefined;
					trace.zmax = undefined;
					trace._zmin = undefined;
					trace._zmax = undefined;
				}
			}
		};
```

